WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:03.150
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.304
- Have y'all, have y'all ever met before?

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:06.137
- No.

00:00:06.137 --> 00:00:07.297
- No, we haven't. I'm
so excited to meet you.

00:00:07.297 --> 00:00:08.130
- Me as well.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:08.963
- It's so nice to meet you.

00:00:08.963 --> 00:00:10.110
- I'm gonna, I'm gonna say

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.360
this is probably the shortest panel

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.700
that we have ever existed with, with me.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.514
- I love this. Now how tall
are you? How tall are you?

00:00:16.514 --> 00:00:18.045
- I'm 5'3 and a 1/2.

00:00:18.045 --> 00:00:18.878
- 5'3 and a 1/2.

00:00:18.878 --> 00:00:19.711
- I'm 5'2.

00:00:19.711 --> 00:00:21.150
- Man, you guys are my goal heights.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.077
- Wait, how tall are you?

00:00:23.077 --> 00:00:24.759
- Wow. I'm 4'11 on a good day.

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:25.592
- Okay.

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:26.425
- I never quite hit 5 feet.

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:27.570
- I'm telling you it's, yeah.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.430
I've never, all of a sudden you walked in,

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:30.510
I was like, I don't know if this is,

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.060
this is us right now.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.240
(panel laughs)

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.470
- Well, Melissa, everyone loved you

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.670
on Big Bang Theory, obviously.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:36.559
It's such a great show.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:37.668
(crowd cheering)

00:00:37.668 --> 00:00:38.760
- Thank you.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:39.690
- What person doesn't love that show?

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.300
So you started out in comedy
doing impressions, right?

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.140
- I did, yeah.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.320
I loved doing any sort of comedy as a kid

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.810
and I was like the kid on the playground

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.240
who didn't really understand recess.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.290
Like, I didn't like I was like,

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.690
this seems exhausting,

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.290
(Kelly laughs)
just the running around.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.067
So, I was like, how do I make
the most use of the time?

00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.900
And I thought maybe I
could like make some coin.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.670
I was always looking
for like, to save money

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.380
to get something at Claire's boutique.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.030
So I was like, well maybe if I like,

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.780
say like, charge 25
cents for an impression,

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.613
I can make money.

00:01:10.613 --> 00:01:11.446
But like,

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:12.720
if kids have pocket change

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.130
they're not gonna spend it on like

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:15.737
a Don Knotts impression

00:01:15.737 --> 00:01:16.570
(audience laughs)

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:17.403
from a seven year-old.

00:01:17.403 --> 00:01:18.268
- Yes.

00:01:18.268 --> 00:01:19.101
- But I would do it anyway.

00:01:19.101 --> 00:01:20.340
Occasionally, like, there were some takers

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.140
and then I, I was shut down eventually

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.160
'cause like my mom got a call

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.200
and they're like, "My
kid didn't buy milk today

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.425
because they said they
bought an Elf impression."

00:01:27.425 --> 00:01:29.784
(everyone laughs)

00:01:29.784 --> 00:01:33.160
- [Kelly] So Melissa, and
here you are as a kid.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:33.993
(crowd cooing)

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.040
Look how cute you are.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.349
Oh, on your, is this one of your shows?

00:01:37.349 --> 00:01:39.900
- Yes. My brother and I would
do shows in our basement.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.460
That was Little Shop of Horrors.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.293
- [Kelly] Nice.

00:01:42.293 --> 00:01:43.620
- We didn't really have a proper stage.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.750
As you can see, that was a lawn chair

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.040
and there was always fear

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.750
that like your foot would fall through.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.550
- Yes. It's one of the
plastic ones with the weave.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.350
- Yeah, exactly. At any point
you could break an ankle.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.183
- [Kelly] Yes.

00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:55.410
- Not the most appropriate
show for a six year-old.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.340
- [Kelly] Probably not, but it's okay-

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.295
- Yeah.

00:01:57.295 --> 00:01:58.128
- Most of it went over our heads.

00:01:58.128 --> 00:01:59.550
- It's true. And to learn at a young age

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.383
that any point

00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:02.160
you could get eaten by a man-eating plant.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.000
- [Kelly] Totally.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:03.833
- It builds character.

00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:04.666
- Yeah.

00:02:04.666 --> 00:02:05.499
It builds fear.

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:06.332
- [Melissa] Exactly.

00:02:06.332 --> 00:02:07.560
- So, we didn't wanna leave Christina out.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.951
So here's, here's Christina
around the same age.

00:02:09.951 --> 00:02:10.784
(audience coos)

00:02:10.784 --> 00:02:12.013
Look at you!

00:02:12.013 --> 00:02:12.846
(Christina laughs)

00:02:12.846 --> 00:02:13.679
- [Christina] My god.

00:02:13.679 --> 00:02:15.122
- [Kelly] I mean, you
ribboned in everything, ma'am.

00:02:15.122 --> 00:02:17.070
- I won. I won something. Yep.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:17.903
- Yeah.

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:19.470
Do you, were you a swimmer?
My kids love swimming.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:20.406
- I was.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.239
- I kept trying to keep them in it.

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:23.520
- Yeah. I swam every
summer on a swim team.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.353
- [Kelly] That's so cool.

00:02:24.353 --> 00:02:25.186
- But not year round.

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:26.741
- Yeah, it's so good for
you. I keep trying to-

00:02:26.741 --> 00:02:29.340
- That's probably why you broke five feet.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.482
That's what I should have been doing.

00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:31.893
(everyone laughs)

00:02:31.893 --> 00:02:32.726
- Right.

00:02:32.726 --> 00:02:33.990
- Just working, just working them out.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.940
So wait, you're now in the new Night Court

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.230
and I love, we were just talking-

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.063
- Yeah.

00:02:38.063 --> 00:02:38.896
- we love the original Night Court.

00:02:38.896 --> 00:02:40.430
So is it similar, similar
to the original show?

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:43.134
- It is. It's very much a
continuation of the original.

00:02:43.134 --> 00:02:45.510
We're calling it a new-boot

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.670
because it's not exactly a reboot.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.480
It, it's very much has the
nostalgia of the original

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.340
and it's grounded in that history.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.230
And we have the amazing
John Larroquette back.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:56.063
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:56.063 --> 00:02:56.896
- Reprising his role as Dan Fielding.

00:02:56.896 --> 00:02:57.729
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- But we also have

00:02:57.729 --> 00:03:00.210
this wonderful new cast
of characters around them.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.770
So, people who aren't familiar

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.647
with the first incarnation,

00:03:03.647 --> 00:03:04.860
they could come to it,

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.710
and needing no prior knowledge, and ...

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:08.543
So, yeah.

00:03:08.543 --> 00:03:10.170
So, it's a new-boot, which we-

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.640
originally when we came up with that term,

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.473
it was a joke

00:03:12.473 --> 00:03:13.306
and then we ended up liking it.

00:03:13.306 --> 00:03:14.139
We were like, all right.

00:03:14.139 --> 00:03:14.997
- [Kelly] I actually
thought it was awesome.

00:03:14.997 --> 00:03:15.830
- Oh, cool.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:16.663
- I heard y'all call it a new-boot

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:17.789
and I was like, how has
no one used that before?

00:03:17.789 --> 00:03:18.720
- That's awesome.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.940
- So, we are all mamas of two kids.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.200
So how old are your kiddos?

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.171
- They're about three and five.

00:03:23.171 --> 00:03:24.210
- Three and five?

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:25.043
- [Christina] Yeah.

00:03:25.043 --> 00:03:25.876
- Okay, mine are six and eight.

00:03:25.876 --> 00:03:27.000
That's not too far off.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.069
- I've got 15 months
and an eight year-old.

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:29.970
- [Melissa] Oh wow.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.020
- 15 months and an eight year-old.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:31.890
So different.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:32.760
You're carrying the-

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.857
you're carrying the bag again

00:03:33.857 --> 00:03:34.690
(audience laughs)

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:35.523
with all the junk.

00:03:35.523 --> 00:03:36.356
- Yeah. You got the-

00:03:36.356 --> 00:03:37.290
- Like a Sherpa.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.600
- Oh yeah. You should see my car.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:39.711
(Kelly giggles)
(crowd cheering)

00:03:39.711 --> 00:03:41.940
- So, we all have kiddos with similar ages

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.230
so we thought it might be nice

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:44.880
to take a moment for ourselves

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.440
and all the other rad mamas out there

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.970
by connecting over coffee

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.340
and answering a few mama
questions from our viewers.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.299
This is A Rad Mama Moment.

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:55.560
(upbeat music)

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.240
Alright, thank you Marcel.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.075
Give it up for Marcel. Giving us some-

00:03:59.075 --> 00:04:00.167
(audience cheers)

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:01.830
I like it.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.480
Oh, this is mine? Okay.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.313
So wait.

00:04:04.313 --> 00:04:05.730
Okay, I think this is yours
Melissa. Okay there we go.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:06.570
- Oh, this is so nice.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:07.403
- This is for you.

00:04:07.403 --> 00:04:08.236
- Wow.

00:04:08.236 --> 00:04:09.899
- Take it or don't,
but I love warm drinks.

00:04:09.899 --> 00:04:10.763
- This is lovely.
- Yep.

00:04:10.763 --> 00:04:11.820
- This is so nice.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.912
- I'm like Elf when it
comes to tea and coffee.

00:04:13.912 --> 00:04:14.760
(audience laughs)

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.590
So wait, so we have our refreshment.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.450
So, now here's our first viewer question.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:19.320
Are you ready?

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.203
- Let's do it.

00:04:20.203 --> 00:04:21.036
- [Kelly] Ber ber ber.
- Yeah.

00:04:21.036 --> 00:04:22.890
- Sherene from Chicago asks,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.040
My kids are at very different stages,

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.190
but sometimes the older one
asks complicated questions

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:30.690
when the younger is around.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.450
What are some harder questions
you've had to deal with

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.330
from your older kids in
front of the younger ones?

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:37.380
- Well, I'm outta this question

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.000
because Cleo, my 15 month old,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.044
has no idea what's going on.

00:04:40.044 --> 00:04:40.877
(audience laughs)
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:40.877 --> 00:04:41.978
- [Christina] So there's
nothing to hide from her.

00:04:41.978 --> 00:04:43.680
- [Kelly] Damn it. You got a pass. Okay.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.063
- I'm out. Ladies?

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:47.760
- You know my question.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.310
The things that that keep on bumping me

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:51.450
are the fact that

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:54.180
I feel like I should
have paid attention more

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.110
in elementary school.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.786
Like if the teacher
had, would've said like,

00:04:56.786 --> 00:05:00.690
learn this because at some
point your kid's gonna ask this.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:01.523
- [Kelly] Yes.

00:05:01.523 --> 00:05:03.390
- And you're not gonna
wanna look like a dummy.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:04.223
- Yeah.

00:05:04.223 --> 00:05:05.670
- Because now like they're,
the other day it was like,

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:06.600
how are clouds made?

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.999
I'm like, water droplets
turn into water vapor

00:05:09.999 --> 00:05:10.832
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:10.832 --> 00:05:11.665
and it goes into the sky.

00:05:11.665 --> 00:05:12.540
And I'm like, I wish.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.550
And then I'm like, Googling secretly,

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.140
and then reading it as if I know.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.160
- I Google openly.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.386
I'm like, let's find out.

00:05:18.386 --> 00:05:19.219
(audience laughs)

00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:20.052
And I'm like, I-

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:20.885
- [Melissa] That's good.
(audience claps)

00:05:20.885 --> 00:05:21.718
- That's our whole thing is we like,

00:05:21.718 --> 00:05:22.551
I go, let's see what,

00:05:22.551 --> 00:05:23.850
you know, I know what it is,

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.280
but somebody else can explain
this better than I can.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.113
- [Melissa] Totally.

00:05:27.113 --> 00:05:27.990
- So I, I Google openly.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:28.823
- It's a good-

00:05:28.823 --> 00:05:29.656
- [Kelly] With no shame.

00:05:29.656 --> 00:05:30.489
- That's great.

00:05:30.489 --> 00:05:31.873
- Yeah, me and Freddie definitely

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:33.833
go online for things.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:34.666
- Yes.

00:05:34.666 --> 00:05:36.420
- My parents, they
wouldn't tell us anything.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.430
We had to go check the encyclopedias.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:39.263
- Yes.
- So, they were like,

00:05:39.263 --> 00:05:40.890
what did we buy all
these encyclopedias for

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.030
if we're just gonna tell you everything?

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.970
So, I'm like, well, let's use Safari.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.129
- Oh my God. Remember those encyclopedia?

00:05:47.129 --> 00:05:48.112
- Oh yeah.

00:05:48.112 --> 00:05:49.440
- We had a whole set.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.273
We had to do our own research.

00:05:50.273 --> 00:05:51.106
(audience claps)

00:05:51.106 --> 00:05:52.710
- I, I never had those,
but I always wanted them.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.767
My friends had, those were cool.

00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:54.870
- They were useful.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:55.962
- Here's the next one though.

00:05:55.962 --> 00:05:58.200
It's from Lisa in Brooklyn and she says,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.700
I have a five year-old

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.010
and am pregnant with my second.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.930
How do you prepare your older child

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.880
for a new baby coming?

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.580
Ooh. How did you specifically do that?

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:10.260
Because they're pretty different ages.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.230
- Well, Freddie has
always wanted a sibling

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.460
and begged me for one.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:15.877
So, I was just like,

00:06:15.877 --> 00:06:17.162
"You're getting a baby!"

00:06:17.162 --> 00:06:19.050
(everyone laughs)

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.853
- Congratulations.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:23.527
- You get a baby, you get a baby.

00:06:23.527 --> 00:06:24.900
(audience titters)

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.633
And yeah, he, he was pretty fine about it.

00:06:28.473 --> 00:06:30.960
He didn't really understand

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.450
how much attention the
baby would be needing.

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:34.650
And now he's sort of,

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:35.880
he'll watch us with her

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:36.877
and then he'll say,

00:06:36.877 --> 00:06:38.280
"So, when I was a baby,

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.033
did I get this much attention?"

00:06:40.033 --> 00:06:41.921
And I'm like, yes, you're alive. Right?

00:06:41.921 --> 00:06:42.892
(everyone laughs)

00:06:42.892 --> 00:06:43.725
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:43.725 --> 00:06:45.150
- Yeah. You, you were followed around.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.920
Made sure you didn't harm yourself.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:47.940
You were fed.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:48.810
- [Kelly] You're doing all right

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:49.643
- Yeah.

00:06:49.643 --> 00:06:50.940
- How did you do that with your-

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.730
- You know, we had read
that it was good to give

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.610
the older child a gift from the baby

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.140
when you came home with the baby.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.250
We did it,

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.337
but my kid was like,

00:07:00.337 --> 00:07:02.833
"Mmm, the baby got me a gift?"

00:07:02.833 --> 00:07:03.666
(everyone laughs)

00:07:03.666 --> 00:07:05.892
"Did the baby go and drive to get me a-

00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:08.553
really, what money did the
baby buy this gift with?

00:07:08.553 --> 00:07:11.100
This seems like a fabrication.
I'm not on board for it."

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:11.933
- [Kelly] It's lies.

00:07:11.933 --> 00:07:12.766
- Exactly. Exactly.

00:07:12.766 --> 00:07:13.830
- My kids, this is hilarious.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:14.940
So they, they're six and eight

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.310
and they want me to have
another baby, right?

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.800
I'm like, like I don't
have enough going on.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.353
And I, and I'm, I'm like,

00:07:21.353 --> 00:07:23.220
I don't really think that's gonna happen,

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.450
but we can get a puppy.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:25.590
And so I got a puppy.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.570
But then their older sister

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:30.000
just had her first child and-

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.290
'cause she's quite a bit older-

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.060
and they went and they were so excited.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.770
I was like, get your
baby stuff out right now

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.510
you're gonna go see
her, and her little one.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.343
I was like, get it out.

00:07:37.343 --> 00:07:39.102
My daughter was like, "Oh my God."

00:07:39.102 --> 00:07:40.290
She's so excited.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.150
And literally like four days later

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.479
she came back to me and was like,

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:45.510
"You're not allowed to have a baby."

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:46.641
- [Christina] Whoa.

00:07:46.641 --> 00:07:48.127
- She was like, she was like,

00:07:48.127 --> 00:07:50.490
"You can't, they just, they cry a lot."

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:51.960
The baby, I guess didn't attach to her

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.160
quite how she (giggles) wanted.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:53.993
- Oh.

00:07:53.993 --> 00:07:55.740
- So, she just, it was hilarious.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:58.462
And I need to send a thank
you gift to her sister.

00:07:58.462 --> 00:07:59.295
(everyone laughs)

00:07:59.295 --> 00:08:00.128
'Cause I was like,

00:08:00.128 --> 00:08:01.710
Mama's not having another baby right now.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:02.543
So, okay.

00:08:02.543 --> 00:08:04.650
So, we have to take
another commercial break

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.204
because I need to stop talking.

00:08:06.204 --> 00:08:07.037
(audience laughs)

00:08:07.037 --> 00:08:09.120
A new episode of Yellowjackets

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.830
premieres this Friday on Showtime

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:13.350
and Night Court airs on Tuesdays on NBC

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:15.150
and streams the next day on Peacock.

